// endpoint_roll_out_build.proto — RollOutBuild: the deploy tooling's way to
// put a build it has ALREADY PRODUCED into service. DAEMON ADMIN section.
//
// THIS IS HOW A DEPLOY REACHES A RUNNING STACK. The deploy chain builds with
// its bounce turned off and then calls this verb; the daemon rolls the build
// out the way it rolls out a self-merge — a blue-green handover for a daemon
// change, a per-workspace shim relaunch for a shim change, a webview reload
// for a webapp change — and every one of those waits for the workspace's
// FREENESS (no turn in flight, no live detached work). NOTHING A ROLLOUT DOES
// ENDS A TURN. That is the whole difference from UpdateShutdownSchedule{now},
// which forces every session down and is an operator's emergency stop, never
// a deploy step.
//
// THE CALLER STATES WHAT IT REBUILT, and the daemon does not work it out. A
// self-merge has a landed commit range to classify; a deploy has none that
// can be trusted — the chain fingerprints ARTIFACTS, so it sees a dirty tree
// and a forced rebuild that no commit range describes. Only the chain knows
// which artifacts moved, so the chain says so.
//
// THE ANSWER IS THE ROLLOUT'S ACCEPTANCE, NOT ITS COMPLETION. A rollout waits
// on freeness for as long as freeness takes — a workspace mid-turn is waited
// on, never interrupted — so completion has no bound this call could hold
// open. What follows arrives on the surfaces that already carry it: a
// handover on WatchDaemon's shutdown_announced (with the successor's
// address), a relaunch on the host stream's shim_attached, a reload on the
// host stream's reload_webapp.

